{"schema_version":1,"course":"tourist-sv","unit":"accommodation-and-shopping","language":"en","target_language":"sv","phrases":[{"concept":"i-have-a-reservation","text":{"Latn":"I have a reservation"}},{"concept":"how-much-is-this","text":{"Latn":"How much is this?"}},{"concept":"too-expensive","text":{"Latn":"That's too expensive"}},{"concept":"do-you-take-cards","text":{"Latn":"Do you accept cards?"}},{"concept":"where-is-the-atm","text":{"Latn":"Where is the ATM?"}},{"concept":"do-you-have-a-free-room","text":{"Latn":"Do you have a free room?"}},{"concept":"the-key-please","text":{"Latn":"The key, please"}},{"concept":"what-time-do-you-open","text":{"Latn":"What time do you open?"}},{"concept":"can-i-have-a-receipt","text":{"Latn":"Can I have a receipt?"}},{"concept":"ill-take-it","text":{"Latn":"I'll take it"}}],"glosses":[{"lemma":"sv:jag","gloss":"I","note":"“Mig” is the object form: “Hjälp mig” (help me)."},{"lemma":"sv:ha","gloss":"to have"},{"lemma":"sv:en","gloss":"a, an; one","note":"Swedish has two genders: “en” for most words (en kaffe) and “ett” for the rest (ett glas). Learn each noun with its article."},{"lemma":"sv:bokning","gloss":"booking, reservation"},{"lemma":"sv:vad","gloss":"what"},{"lemma":"sv:kosta","gloss":"to cost"},{"lemma":"sv:det","gloss":"it, that"},{"lemma":"sv:har","gloss":"here","note":"“Det här” is “this”, literally “the here”."},{"lemma":"sv:vara","gloss":"to be","note":"“Är” is the present tense for every person: jag är, du är, det är."},{"lemma":"sv:for-2","gloss":"too (excessively)","note":"The same word as the preposition “för”; before an adjective it means “too”."},{"lemma":"sv:dyr","gloss":"expensive"},{"lemma":"sv:ta","gloss":"to take","note":"“Hur tar jag mig till…?” is literally “how take I myself to…?”, the usual way to ask the way."},{"lemma":"sv:ni","gloss":"you (plural or formal)","note":"Used with shop and hotel staff when you want to be polite; “du” is also fine."},{"lemma":"sv:emot","gloss":"(in “ta emot”) to accept","note":"“Ta emot” is a phrasal verb: “Tar ni emot kort?” is “Do you accept cards?”"},{"lemma":"sv:kort","gloss":"card"},{"lemma":"sv:var","gloss":"where"},{"lemma":"sv:finnas","gloss":"to exist; to be (located)","note":"“Var finns…?” asks where something is, literally “where is there…?”."},{"lemma":"sv:bankomat","gloss":"cash machine (ATM)"},{"lemma":"sv:ledig","gloss":"free, vacant"},{"lemma":"sv:rum","gloss":"room"},{"lemma":"sv:nyckel","gloss":"key"},{"lemma":"sv:tack","gloss":"thank you; please","note":"Means “thank you”, and after a request also “please”: “En kaffe, tack.”"},{"lemma":"sv:nar","gloss":"when"},{"lemma":"sv:oppna","gloss":"to open"},{"lemma":"sv:kunna","gloss":"can, to be able to"},{"lemma":"sv:fa","gloss":"to get, to receive; may","note":"“Kan jag få…?” is the polite way to ask for something."},{"lemma":"sv:kvitto","gloss":"receipt"}]}
